Crossover Cable: One end wired as T568A and opposite end wired as T568B (pins 1,2 swap with pins 3,6: Tx pairs connect to Rx pairs). Connects similar/like devices (DTE to DTE / DCE to DCE): PC to PC, Switch to Switch, Router to PC.
3. EXPLANATION / PROCEDURE
Tools Required: CAT6 UTP Cable, RJ45 Modular Plugs, Wire Stripper, Modular Crimping Tool, LAN Cable Continuity Tester.
Step-by-Step Crimping Procedure:
Step 1 (Stripping): Use cable stripper to carefully score and remove ~2 cm (0.75 in) of outer PVC jacket without nicking the copper wires.
Step 2 (Untwisting & Straightening): Cut internal plastic spline; untwist wire pairs down to jacket edge; flatten and straighten conductors.
Step 3 (Color Ordering): Align conductors strictly into the T568B sequence: White-Orange, Orange, White-Green, Blue, White-Blue, Green, White-Brown, Brown.
Step 4 (Trimming): Hold wires flat in order and trim evenly perpendicular with crimper wire cutter, leaving ~1.2 cm exposed.
Step 5 (Insertion): Push wires into RJ45 connector with pins facing UP until all 8 conductors touch the copper end contacts and the outer jacket passes beyond the strain-relief clamp.
Step 6 (Crimping): Insert assembly into 8P socket of crimping tool and squeeze handles firmly. Contact teeth pierce wire insulation while strain-relief wedge locks the jacket.
Step 7 (Testing): Insert both cable ends into master and remote ports of LAN Cable Tester. Verify that LEDs 1 through 8 illuminate in sequential ascending order without open, short, or crossed circuits.
4. CONCLUSION
Successfully fabricated and tested CAT6 Ethernet patch cables using RJ45 connectors. Verified T568B wiring pinout continuity using a LAN cable tester and understood the structural distinction between Straight-Through and Crossover cable applications. -->
